From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mikhail Zinchenko <m1d3ry@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Skip the delay-load failure hook when delayimp.h is
 missing

`Exceptions.cpp` installs a delay-load failure hook on every Windows build, and the hook needs
`<delayimp.h>`. Visual Studio always ships the header. A toolchain that exposes only part of the MSVC
include directory may not. hermetic-llvm is one example: it copies an allowlist of MSVC headers into
the build, and `delayimp.h` is not on it. The file then fails with "file not found".

The hook only matters for a binary linked with `/DELAYLOAD`. Upstream CMake passes that flag for the
two WinRT DLLs that bundle support needs. A build without it never runs the hook.

Compile the hook only when `__has_include` finds the header. When the header is present, the hook
compiles as before. A compiler without `__has_include` drops it, which is safe for the same reason.
---
 src/msix/common/Exceptions.cpp | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/msix/common/Exceptions.cpp b/src/msix/common/Exceptions.cpp
index 3fafd459..939bf00f 100644
--- a/src/msix/common/Exceptions.cpp
+++ b/src/msix/common/Exceptions.cpp
@@ -4,7 +4,16 @@
 // 
 #include "Exceptions.hpp"
 
-#ifdef WIN32
+// The delay-load failure hook needs <delayimp.h>. Visual Studio always ships it, but a toolchain that
+// exposes only part of the MSVC include directory may not. The hook only matters for a binary linked
+// with /DELAYLOAD, so skip it when the header is missing.
+#if defined(WIN32) && defined(__has_include)
+#if __has_include(<delayimp.h>)
+#define MSIX_HAS_DELAYIMP 1
+#endif
+#endif
+
+#if defined(WIN32) && defined(MSIX_HAS_DELAYIMP)
 #include <delayimp.h>
 
 // Custom failure hook that will be called if delayloading a dll fails. This give us the ability
